{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2006, the International Astronomical \nUnion passed its now-infamous resolution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Pluto is not a planet. In fact, \nthe resolution didn't just demote Pluto,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9200,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but laid out a list of three criteria you must \npass to be considered a planet. Whether or not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you agree with the IAU’s definition (and I don't), \nit's possible to argue that – according to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "criteria in their definition, our Moon also \nqualifies as a planet! Here’s that argument. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll start with criteria A: “A planet is a \ncelestial body that: a) is in orbit around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27600,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun” – And while we often think of the moon \nas orbiting the Earth – I mean, from the Earth’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31360,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perspective, it is – from the Sun’s perspective, \nthe moon and earth together orbit the Sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nudging each other in and out as they go. In \nfact, as I’ve explored in a previous video, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39200,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational force from the Sun on the Moon is \ntwice as much as the force from the Earth and so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon’s orbit always curves inwards towards the \nSun, never outwards towards the Earth. So. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47520,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible to argue that the Moon meets criteria \nA: it’s a celestial body in orbit around the Sun. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52000,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now, criteria B: “A planet is a celestial \nbody that: b) has sufficient mass for its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56520,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "self-gravity to overcome rigid body forces \nso that it assumes a hydrostatic equilibrium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61360,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(or nearly round) shape.” It’s fairly \nevident that the moon is, like the earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a round sphere and not an irregularly-shaped \nobject like many smaller asteroids, comets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69760,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and other moons that don't have enough gravity to \npull themselves into rounded shapes. The moon not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only meets this second criteria, it meets it by \na long shot – the moon is many times more massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than other gravitationally rounded objects in \nour solar system like Pluto, the asteroid Ceres,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. In short, the Moon meets criteria B: it has \nsufficient mass to be gravitationally rounded. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we come to criteria C: “A planet is a celestial \nbody that: c) has cleared the neighbourhood around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its orbit.” This criteria is asking for a planet \nto have enough mass relative to its orbital radius  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it gravitationally dominates everything \nnearby: a planet should either kick other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100800,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things out of its orbit or control their motion \nwithin it, like, by capturing them as a moon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104640,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or as a trojan asteroid, or pulling \nthem to crash into the planet itself.. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This third criteria is the one that disqualifies \nPluto from the IAU’s blessing as a planet. Here’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112880,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a plot of the masses of various objects in our \nsolar system and the distances of their orbits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the sun – anything above the diagonal line is \nbig enough to “clear its orbit”. As you can see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pluto is too far from the sun – given its size \n– to dominate other objects, and is itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually gravitationally dominated by Neptune. \nInterestingly, if Pluto were as close to the sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as Mercury, it would be able to clear its orbit, \nand if the earth moved into the outer Kuiper belt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134160,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it wouldn’t be able to clear its orbit, but more \nimportantly, look here – the moon is well and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clearly positioned in the “big enough to clear its \norbit” zone! If the Earth suddenly disappeared,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon would have no issue being considered a \nplanet. Except there would be no one left to care. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, the moon ticks all three of the IAU’s \nplanet boxes. Why then, you might ask, why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn’t the IAU celebrate the moon as a planet? \nLook closer at their definition of a planet and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll get your answer: there are two more hidden \ncriteria! Just before the three primary criteria,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see that the IAU definition applies to “planets \nand other bodies, except satellites”. I’m calling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this “criteria X” because the IAU didn't label it:\nCriteria X: the object must not be a satellite of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another object – that is, not a moon.\nAh. Well I guess that explains it. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176960,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moon is, after all, a moon. \nBut there’s one final nail in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon’s IAU-planethood coffin – a footnote! \nReally? Down here at the bottom it says: “The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eight planets are: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, \nJupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune.” Too bad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189360,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon isn’t on the list. But… what was the \npoint of a definition if they were just going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192640,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to list what they count as planets anyway? Why \nnot just publish the list and be done with it? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This footnote totally reveals the underlying \nstory behind the IAU’s definition of planethood:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201120,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make a list in advance of what objects \nthey thought should be called planets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and after the fact try to find scientific-sounding \njustifications for the list. Which to me is fairly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-scientific! Instead of being descriptive \n(that is, looking at what exists and seeing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how it naturally breaks into categories), the \nIAU definition is prescriptive: it just says  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217360,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"these things on our list are planets.\"\nIf they were at least honest about that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I wouldn’t have as much of a problem. Ultimately, \n\"planet\" is a human concept that serves human  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224720,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs. It wouldn't be that ridiculous to \njust write down a list and say \"these are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228400,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the objects in our solar system that we all agree \nto call planets because human culture and history  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232080,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "values them as planets.\" And if we wanted, Pluto \ncould be on that list, or not. And the moon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or not. And the asteroid Ceres, or not. But I’m \nnot a fan of pretending that an arbitrary list has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241360,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientific justification behind it; and as we’ve \nseen here, the supposedly scientific definition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245120,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is pretty poorly thought out, and finally, I \npersonally don’t think we should just use an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arbitrary list: science pushes us to re-evaluate \nour preconceptions and to try to use labels whose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning has a basis in the natural world rather \nthan human whims. And according to the best,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257120,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most comprehensive modern planetary science: \nthe IAU’s list of planets is wrong. According  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to planetary science, the moon is also a planet. \nThe asteroid Ceres is also a planet! The big round  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moons of Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune \nare planets! And of course, Pluto is a planet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all that is a story for another day. \nPs there’s another hilarious problem with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277440,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the IAU’s definition of planet, which \nwe’ll get to in a second, but first… "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280800,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Creating these videos is possible thanks to \neveryone who supports MinutePhysics on Patreon:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284800,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your help allows me the freedom to create videos \nwithout worrying as much about sponsorships and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288560,
    "dDurationMs": 3587,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so on. As a year-end thank you, I’m sending \nevery active patreon supporter a print of an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292147,
    "dDurationMs": 3933,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "illustration from the most viewed MinutePhysics \nvideo this year: “The Magnetic Shadow Effect” If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ve enjoyed watching my videos, please consider \nsupporting me on patreon - thank you so much!\u2028Ok,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301360,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so another problem with the IAU’s definition \nfor a planet is that if a small red dwarf star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305360,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passed by the solar system and got captured and \nwent into orbit around the sun… according to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "IAU’s definition, that star would also be a \nplanet. Even though it’s a star! And people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313600,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know this is a problem because definitions for \nexoplanets often say they have to be substellar"
    } ]
  } ]
}
